The routine sin-f computes the sine of a whole number of degrees, from a table every 5 degrees, interpolated
without a loop.

Result: 1000 sin x rounded to nearest, for x a whole number of degrees from 0 to 359, held to -999..999. A cell
holds three digits, so STO would keep 1000 as 000; the result is capped at 999 instead, which makes sin 89, 90
and 91 all 999 and sin 269, 270 and 271 all -999.

Call: load x into the accumulator and JMP 18. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 18-98 (81 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 48-49, 52-53, 58-60, 89, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 52 instructions a call, 45.5 on average over the domain, counted from its first cell to its return
jump. Worst error: 1 in the last digit (thousandths) against the correctly rounded value, at x = 1; 88 of the 360
inputs are off.
Address bases: cell 56 holds ADD 62; cell 57 holds JMP 88. To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from
every address in its instructions that points into cells 18-98 and from the address bases; addresses 00 and 99
stay as they are.

